KG42.register("rules-f4ebe437547151901cb5c94a",{"id":"38-bidirectional_ev_trading::v141-c-R003","paper":"38-bidirectional_ev_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"M01","name":"Price-signal coordination","process_stage":"coordination","market_meaning":"The aggregator sets and applies electricity rates to coordinate customers' decentralized, utility-maximizing energy schedules. Scope: Collaborative distributed energy scheduling under the linear billing scheme represented by problems P2_i. The coordinating electricity rates are identical across customers and are determined solely to maximize social welfare, without considering allocation of total cost between customers and the aggregator. Scope: Collaborative linear billing with optimal electricity rates. Each customer's distributed scheduling iteration needs only the wholesale price function and the sum of other customers' energy consumption for every time-slot. Scope: Each iteration of the collaborative Gauss-Seidel distributed scheduling algorithm.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function coordination.price_signal under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.local_operator","coordination.price_signal"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E001","source":"coordination.local_operator","target":"coordination.price_signal","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Collaborative distributed energy scheduling under the linear billing scheme represented by problems P2_i. | Collaborative linear billing with optimal electricity rates. | Each iteration of the collaborative Gauss-Seidel distributed scheduling algorithm.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g02-e04"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The aggregator sets and applies electricity rates to coordinate customers' decentralized, utility-maximizing energy schedules. Scope: Collaborative distributed energy scheduling under the linear billing scheme represented by problems P2_i.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The coordinating electricity rates are identical across customers and are determined solely to maximize social welfare, without considering allocation of total cost between customers and the aggregator. Scope: Collaborative linear billing with optimal electricity rates.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each customer's distributed scheduling iteration needs only the wholesale price function and the sum of other customers' energy consumption for every time-slot. Scope: Each iteration of the collaborative Gauss-Seidel distributed scheduling algorithm.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Energy Scheduling: Collaborative Approach","anchor_text":"ch and function C ′t(z) denotes the ﬁrst derivative of the totaltal cost function Ct(z) with respect","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"From Proposition 1, we can interpret problem P2_i, \\forall i\\in I as a distributed energy scheduling framework based on a linear billing scheme where the aggregator decides the electricity rates \\bar{\\pi}^*=[\\pi^{*t}]_{\\forall t\\in T}, and apply these electricity rates to all customers. Then, each customer i decides its own energy scheduling to maximize its own utility by solving problem P2_i. The aggregator charges each customer an electricity bill according to the announced electricity rates and the amount of energy consumption at each time-slot."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Energy Scheduling: Collaborative Approach","anchor_text":"of how much energy the customers consume. However, itm should be noted that the optimal electricity ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to Proposition 2, the linear billing scheme always guarantees that the aggregator can make a profit regardless of how much energy the customers consume. However, it should be noted that the optimal electricity rates, \\bar{\\pi}^*, are determined only focusing on maximizing the social welfare without consideration on how we distribute the total cost to customers and the aggregator. Moreover, the linear electricity rates, \\bar{\\pi}^*, which are identically applied to all the customers, can result in unfairness among customers with different energy consumption levels. The unfairness issue can be explained as follows. Since the wholesale price, p^t(\\sum_{i\\in I}x_{i,Tot}^{*t}), increases as the total demand at time-slot t, \\sum_{i\\in I}x_{i,Tot}^{*t}, increases, the optimal electricity rate, \\pi^{*t}, also increases as \\sum_{i\\in I}x_{i,Tot}^{*t} increases as shown in (9). Hence, the presence of heavy customers with a large energy consumption drives up the electricity rates, which is unfair to light customers. In the next section, we shall take into consideration the cost allocation and unfairness issues for a non-collaborative scenario."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-54c42059a7-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":"1222","section":"III. Energy Scheduling: Collaborative Approach","anchor_text":"of where Xt,(k+1)−i = i′\u003ci xt,(k+1)i′,T ot + i′>i xt,(k)i′,T ot. Problem sh P1i is a convex optimiza","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Note that at each iteration, each customer needs to acquire only the information about the wholesale price function p^t(\\cdot), \\forall t\\in T and the sum energy consumption of other customers, X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}, \\forall t\\in T. Such information can be updated and broadcast by the aggregator through the communication with the customer's ECS at the beging of each iteration."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":"R003's frozen graph contains only the coordination operator and price signal, with no independently typed decision variable. The quoted R003 evidence describes who sets/applies rates and what information is exchanged, while the customer-schedule variables x_{i,Tot}^t and their feasible set are defined in R001/R005 scope. Declaring them here would import cross-rule algebraic decision objects not represented in R003's frozen members. B evidence is unreviewed.","empty_theta_reason":"The parameters appearing in the broader source (for example a^t, b^t in the wholesale price function and the optimal rate vector bar-pi*) are defined in R001/R005 pricing/clearing evidence, not in R003's frozen members. R003 has no pricing.local or clearing.social_welfare node, and no R003 edge carries those parameters. Declaring them here would change the rule's scope. B evidence is unreviewed."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-settlement"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E14","quote":"the aggregator decides the electricity rates \\bar{\\pi}^*=[\\pi^{*t}]_{\\forall t\\in T}, and apply these electricity rates to all customers. Then, each customer i decides its own energy scheduling to maximize its own utility by solving problem P2_i."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","quote":"the optimal electricity rates, \\bar{\\pi}^*, are determined only focusing on maximizing the social welfare without consideration on how we distribute the total cost to customers and the aggregator."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","quote":"the linear electricity rates, \\bar{\\pi}^*, which are identically applied to all the customers"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","quote":"Note that at each iteration, each customer needs to acquire only the information about the wholesale price function p^t(\\cdot), \\forall t\\in T and the sum energy consumption of other customers, X_{-i}^{t,(k+1)}, \\forall t\\in T."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"77fc763eba2526645daa47dde4cae9ff7bdd330697972a56f00b994c8c93c937","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"R003 is a procedural price-signal coordination rule. Its frozen graph and supplied quotations describe role assignment and information exchange: the aggregator sets and applies common linear electricity rates, customers respond by solving their own scheduling problems, and each iteration requires the wholesale price function and other customers' aggregate consumption. The algebraic rate and schedule formulas appearing in the broader source (e.g. P2_i and eq. (9)) are attached to R001/R005 scope in this assembly and are not represented by an independent variable/constraint on R003's members. B evidence remains unreviewed; no independent source audit is claimed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator","coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R003-E001"],"rationale":"C001 is the role-based linkage represented by the frozen edge from the local coordination operator to the price signal: the aggregator's rate decision/application and the customers' local utility-maximizing responses are the scope of that edge."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 qualifies the price signal itself: the rates are common and chosen solely for social-welfare purposes. The frozen R003 graph has no edge carrying this rate-property constraint; R003-E001 is constrained only by C001, so no edge is attached here."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C003 states the information content of the price-signal coordination step. Its frozen R003 member is coordination.price_signal; no R003 edge is constrained by C003, so the information-exchange statement is bound to the signal node without adding an unrepresented edge."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
